import{_ as t,o,c as r,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Contribution Rules","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"project/contribution-rules.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"project/contribution-rules.md","filePath":"project/contribution-rules.md"}'),a={name:"project/contribution-rules.md"};function n(i,e,l,d,u,c){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="contribution-rules" tabindex="-1">Contribution Rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#contribution-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Contribution Rules&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>How to contribute to code that isn&#39;t yours, with AI, without being the reason a maintainer stops accepting outside pull requests. These rules apply to your team fork, the PR round and, most of all, any upstream PR.</p></blockquote><h2 id="why-these-rules-exist" tabindex="-1">Why these rules exist <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-these-rules-exist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why these rules exist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open-source maintainers spent 2025 and 2026 under a flood of AI-generated pull requests and reports, and many changed their rules in response:</p><ul><li><strong>Policies are now common, and they converge.</strong> Of 118 contributor AI policies found in 1,000 popular repositories, 78% allow AI-assisted contributions, 22% discourage AI use, 51% require disclosure and 74% require a human in the loop (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.16706" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hora and Robbes</a>).</li><li><strong>Some projects decline AI-generated content outright.</strong> QEMU&#39;s policy is to &quot;DECLINE any contributions which are believed to include or derive from AI generated content&quot;, because of copyright and certificate-of-origin uncertainty; using AI for research or debugging is allowed if its output isn&#39;t included (<a href="https://github.com/qemu/qemu/blob/master/docs/devel/code-provenance.rst" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">QEMU</a>). Gentoo bans content created with AI tools, and NetBSD treats LLM output as &quot;presumed to be tainted code&quot; (<a href="https://wiki.gentoo.org/wiki/Project:Council/AI_policy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Gentoo</a>; <a href="https://www.netbsd.org/developers/commit-guidelines.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NetBSD</a>).</li><li><strong>Others allow it with strict conditions.</strong> Ghostty: &quot;All AI usage in any form must be disclosed&quot;, and &quot;If you can&#39;t explain what your changes do… do not contribute&quot;; people who submit low-quality AI work may be put on a public list that &quot;will block all future contributions&quot; (<a href="https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/blob/main/AI_POLICY.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ghostty</a>). LLVM: &quot;Contributors must read and review all LLM-generated code&quot;, new contributors should &quot;start with small contributions&quot;, disclosure uses an <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailer, PR descriptions are written by the contributor, and agents acting &quot;without human approval&quot; are banned (<a href="https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/llvm/docs/AIToolPolicy.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LLVM</a>). Django asks contributors to disclose their tools, verify output against the full test suite, and not to request automated AI reviews on PRs, because they &quot;often generate noise&quot; (<a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/internals/contributing/writing-code/submitting-patches/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Django</a>). The Linux kernel&#39;s rule: &quot;AI agents MUST NOT add Signed-off-by tags&quot;, and the human takes full responsibility (<a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/Documentation/process/coding-assistants.rst" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">kernel</a>).</li><li><strong>The cost of low-effort contributions is real.</strong> curl ended its bug bounty to remove the incentive for AI-generated junk reports (<a href="https://www.theregister.com/2026/01/21/curl_ends_bug_bounty/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">The Register</a>); Hacktoberfest dropped its pull-request challenge after &quot;a massive flood of activity and burnout from the rise of low-effort PRs&quot; (<a href="https://hacktoberfest.com/llms.txt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hacktoberfest</a>); and GitHub added settings that let repositories restrict or cap PRs from outside contributors (<a href="https://github.com/orgs/community/discussions/187038" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>).</li><li><strong>One person&#39;s behavior can close a door for a whole institution.</strong> In 2021 the Linux kernel banned contributions from the University of Minnesota after a research group submitted deliberately flawed &quot;hypocrite commits&quot; (<a href="https://www.theregister.com/2021/04/21/minnesota_linux_kernel_flaws_update/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">The Register</a>; <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/854076/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LWN</a>).</li><li><strong>Policies change fast.</strong> QEMU has a pending proposal to allow small AI-assisted changes recorded with its own <code>AI-used-for:</code> trailer, explicitly not <code>Assisted-by</code> (<a href="https://patchew.org/QEMU/20260529094619.1034458-1-pbonzini@redhat.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">patch</a>); the kernel&#39;s master policy no longer mentions <code>Assisted-by</code> at all (<a href="https://hackaday.com/2026/10/15/linux-kernel-drops-ai-tag-requirement-uses-meta-ai-for-code-reviews/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hackaday</a>). Always read the current text, not a summary.</li></ul><p>The rules on this page are what these projects ask for, collected. They&#39;re also the course&#39;s own rules everywhere, including your team fork.</p><h2 id="the-rules" tabindex="-1">The rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rules&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="before-you-write-any-code" tabindex="-1">Before you write any code <a class="header-anchor" href="#before-you-write-any-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Before you write any code&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Read the project&#39;s policies first:</strong> its <code>CONTRIBUTING</code> file, code of conduct, and AI policy if it has one. Write down in your PR&#39;s AI section which policy applies and what it requires.</li><li><strong>The stricter policy wins.</strong> If the project&#39;s AI policy is stricter than the course&#39;s, follow the project&#39;s. If it declines AI-assisted contributions, don&#39;t send it AI-assisted work, however small. If it uses its own disclosure format (a different trailer, a checkbox, a sentence), use that instead of, or as well as, <code>Assisted-by:</code>.</li><li><strong>Issue first.</strong> Comment on the issue (or open one) before you open a PR, say what you plan to do, and wait for a maintainer&#39;s response where the project expects it. Don&#39;t take an issue someone else is working on.</li></ol><h3 id="the-change" tabindex="-1">The change <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The change&quot;">​</a></h3><ol start="4"><li><strong>Small and focused.</strong> One concern per PR. LLVM asks new contributors to start small, and course data agree: students &quot;should make smaller PRs&quot; (<a href="https://blog.humphd.org/teaching-open-source-fall-2021/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Seneca OSD600</a>).</li><li><strong>You can explain every line.</strong> If you can&#39;t explain what a line does and why it&#39;s there, it doesn&#39;t go in the PR. &quot;If you can&#39;t explain it, you didn&#39;t build it.&quot;</li><li><strong>Tested to the project&#39;s standard:</strong> the full test suite passes locally, new behavior has tests, and the project&#39;s linters and formatters are clean.</li><li><strong>License and provenance.</strong> No code copied from elsewhere unless its license is compatible and you say where it came from. LLM output can reproduce existing open-source code closely: in one study even the best models produced near-copies in 0.88–2.01% of outputs and often got the license wrong, especially for copyleft code (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.02487" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LiCoEval</a>).</li></ol><h3 id="the-pr" tabindex="-1">The PR <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pr" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The PR&quot;">​</a></h3><ol start="8"><li><strong>A description you wrote yourself</strong> (🔴 AI off): what, why, how you verified it, and the risks. LLVM asks for the same.</li><li><strong>Disclosure:</strong> the AI section of the <a href="./../templates/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.html">PR template</a> (or the project&#39;s own form), and <code>Assisted-by: [tool and model]</code> on AI-assisted commits unless the project uses another format. Detection tools can&#39;t reliably tell agent-written commits from human ones, so recorded attribution is the only reliable record (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36229" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Horikawa et al.</a>).</li><li><strong>You sign, never an agent.</strong> Where a project uses a Developer Certificate of Origin (<code>Signed-off-by:</code>), only you add it, and only if you can honestly certify it for AI-assisted work under that project&#39;s policy.</li><li><strong>No unattended agents in other people&#39;s spaces.</strong> No agent opens issues, PRs or comments in another team&#39;s fork or upstream on its own. Don&#39;t summon AI bots on other people&#39;s PRs, and don&#39;t request automated AI reviews where a project asks you not to.</li></ol><h3 id="after-you-open-it" tabindex="-1">After you open it <a class="header-anchor" href="#after-you-open-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;After you open it&quot;">​</a></h3><ol start="12"><li><strong>Answer review yourself.</strong> Read the feedback, think, then change the code or reply with a reason. LLVM&#39;s policy: &quot;Passing maintainer feedback to an LLM doesn&#39;t help anyone grow.&quot;</li><li><strong>One open upstream PR per student per project.</strong> Some projects, such as the MCP TypeScript SDK, already allow outside contributors only one open PR at a time (<a href="./../research/notes/tools-senior.html">tools notes</a>).</li><li><strong>Be patient and kind.</strong> A closed PR is a normal outcome, not an insult. Don&#39;t ping maintainers repeatedly, and don&#39;t argue a &quot;no&quot;. In Michigan&#39;s EECS 481, 44% of student PRs were accepted before AI made maintainers warier (<a href="https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~weimerw/p/weimer-fse2023.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Fang et al.</a>), and rejections often have reasons that have nothing to do with you: in one study of agent PRs, only 35.7% of rejections were clear failures of the change (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22534" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Peralta et al.</a>).</li><li><strong>No research on maintainers.</strong> Never submit a PR, issue or comment as an experiment, and never study how maintainers respond to you. That is human-subjects research and needs IRB approval (<a href="./../graduate/empirical-study.html#no-human-subjects-research">graduate study rules</a>).</li></ol><h2 id="the-three-tiers-and-what-applies-where" tabindex="-1">The three tiers, and what applies where <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-three-tiers-and-what-applies-where" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The three tiers, and what applies where&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Team fork (Tier 1)</th><th>PR round (Tier 2)</th><th>Upstream (Tier 3)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Read the policies; stricter wins</td><td>The parent project&#39;s policy is the reference; the course&#39;s applies</td><td>The receiving team&#39;s <code>AGENTS.md</code> and conventions</td><td>The project&#39;s policy, in full</td></tr><tr><td>Issue first</td><td>Your team&#39;s issues</td><td>An <code>eswa-pr-round</code> issue in the receiving team&#39;s fork</td><td>An upstream issue, with a maintainer&#39;s response where expected</td></tr><tr><td>Self-written description, disclosure, trailers</td><td>Required</td><td>Required</td><td>Required, in the project&#39;s format</td></tr><tr><td>One open PR at a time</td><td>—</td><td>One PR per student</td><td>One per student per project</td></tr><tr><td>Staff screening</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Required for your first upstream PR</td></tr><tr><td>Graded</td><td>Yes (milestones)</td><td>Yes (PR round rubric)</td><td>Bonus only</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="optional-upstream-prs-tier-3" tabindex="-1">Optional upstream PRs (Tier 3) <a class="header-anchor" href="#optional-upstream-prs-tier-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Optional upstream PRs (Tier 3)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From week 12 to week 15 you may offer one change to the parent of the class codebase, or to a project on the short pre-vetted list the instructor posts on Canvas. Every project on that list has a written policy that allows disclosed AI assistance, recent commits and responsive maintainers, labeled good-first issues, a maintainer contact made before the term, and no history of capping or closing outside PRs.</p><ol><li><strong>Do the work in your team fork first.</strong> An upstream PR is a port of a change that has already been reviewed by a teammate and passed CI in your fork. Course documents (<code>course/</code>) never go upstream.</li><li><strong>Get it screened.</strong> Bring your first upstream PR to staff before you open it: in the week 14 Lab, or in office hours from week 12. Staff check the project&#39;s policy, the issue discussion, the size of the diff, the tests, the description and the disclosure.</li><li><strong>Open it yourself</strong> from your own fork, with the project&#39;s template and disclosure format.</li><li><strong>Follow it through</strong> until the end of the term: answer review, revise, or close it politely if the maintainers decline. If the term ends first, say so on the PR and either keep following it or close it.</li></ol><h3 id="how-upstream-work-is-graded" tabindex="-1">How upstream work is graded <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-upstream-work-is-graded" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How upstream work is graded&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Process, not merges.</strong> Nothing in your grade depends on a maintainer&#39;s decision. The change itself is already graded in your team fork.</li><li><strong>A merge earns a small bonus:</strong> one percentage point on your course grade for an upstream PR merged before the University&#39;s grade deadline, at most one such bonus per student. A closed or unmerged PR never costs anything.</li><li><strong>Breaking these rules upstream</strong> (an unscreened first PR, undisclosed AI use, an agent posting on your behalf) is handled as an &quot;explain it or redo it&quot; conversation first, and as academic integrity if it misrepresents your work (<a href="./../assessment/#academic-integrity">how</a>). Tell the instructor at once if something goes wrong upstream; reporting it is never penalized.</li></ul>',22)])])}const f=t(a,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,f as default};
